{
  "requirement": "For requirement and example evidence, both `true` and `FIXME` MUST satisfy the determination check in `habeas::validate()`. A false determination MUST fail validation and be reported with its rationale. Each definition's determination MUST be checked on its own; a false determination MUST NOT fail the definitions it implements, tests, or cites. All other evidence checks MUST still pass: FIXME MUST NOT excuse malformed, missing, or stale evidence or uncovered references. Validation MUST check the recorded outcome, schema, input freshness, and reference coverage without inference or reassessing whether a source comment adequately acknowledges a gap.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The implementation looks correct, but the tests don't check two explicit obligations of the requirement.\n\n**What the implementation does right**\n- `validate_evidence_file` in `src/evidence.rs` (the `{impl}` marker is at about line 637) calls `Judgment::from_value`. That function, in `src/judgment.rs`, accepts `true`, `false`, and `{\"FIXME\": [nonempty strings]}`.\n- Only `Judgment::False` pushes an error. The error reads `\"{label}: determination is false: <rationale>\"`, so a false determination fails validation and carries its rationale.\n- `true` and `FIXME` add no error from this check.\n- Each file is judged only from its own record. Nothing propagates a false result to the definitions that the failing definition implements, tests, or cites.\n- Malformed, stale, or uncovered evidence still produces its own errors regardless of a FIXME. `fixme_does_not_hide_stale_inputs_or_uncovered_references` in `tests/outcomes.rs` checks this.\n\n**What the tests cover**\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (`src/evidence.rs`) asserts that a `false` record produces `determination is false`.\n- `fixme_examples_pass_without_test_references_but_true_examples_do_not` (`tests/outcomes.rs`) checks that FIXME passes, and that `false` fails with `determination is false`.\n- `fixme_requirements_accept_issue_references_and_other_content` and `fixme_fixture` (`tests/commands.rs`) check that FIXME records validate with no errors.\n- The batch tests in `tests/authoring_batch.rs` check that `false` and FIXME records are handled and that the result still validates.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Rationale in the report is untested.** The requirement says a false determination \"MUST … be reported with its rationale.\" I found no assertion on the `determination is false: <rationale>` text.\n   - The evidence-file test uses the rationale \"Work remains.\" but only checks the substring `determination is false`.\n   - The `tests/commands.rs` status test (`status_lists_nonvalid_requirements_and_all_option_in_name_order`) also checks only that substring.\n   - A regression that dropped the rationale from the message would pass every test.\n2. **Independence is not isolated in any test.** The requirement says a false determination \"MUST NOT fail the definitions it implements, tests, or cites,\" and that each is \"checked on its own.\"\n   - The test in `tests/outcomes.rs` that sets the `timeout` example to false only asserts that some error contains `determination is false`. It does not assert that the requirement `ready` (which records `timeout` as an input) stays error-free.\n   - In the status fixture, `pending` is false and `ready` is Valid, but `ready` has no relationship to `pending`.\n   - The evidence-file test makes `requirements.one` false while also seeding many other errors in that record. It cannot show that a related definition is unaffected.\n\nBoth obligations are stated explicitly in the requirement, so the tests miss interesting points in its design space. No FIXME or issue reference acknowledges either gap.",
